3.3 Worker operating journey

Worker registers and submits verification information.

After approval, worker creates the Digital Dukaan and defines service area and operating hours.

Worker adds services, price units, prices, turnaround estimates and capacity.

Customer discovers the shop and submits a request.

Worker reviews details and accepts, rejects or asks for clarification.

Worker confirms price and expected completion; customer accepts revised quote if applicable.

Worker receives items and records count, condition and parcel handoff.

Worker performs the service and updates milestones.

Worker marks the order ready and hands the sealed/labelled parcel to the return partner or customer.

After delivery/collection is confirmed, earnings are posted according to settlement policy.

3.4 Worker controls and guardrails

Worker can change future prices but cannot rewrite historical confirmed price snapshots.

Worker cannot view other workers' bookings or customer records.

Worker cannot mark a parcel as received without a custody/receipt event.

Worker must flag count or condition mismatch at handover rather than silently editing the order.

Worker can pause new bookings while existing accepted jobs remain visible and actionable.

Worker verification documents must not be visible to customers or delivery partners.

Worker payout totals must be traceable to booking-level financial records.

