6.7 Backend transaction and consistency rules

Booking creation must be idempotent and must reserve any limited slot atomically.

Worker acceptance must prevent two workers accepting the same exclusive booking.

Batch creation must lock/check task membership and capacity to prevent overbooking.

Partner acceptance must ensure only one active assignment for a task unless multi-partner task design is explicitly introduced.

Payment success must be based on verified provider events, not a client success screen.

Refunds and payout adjustments must create traceable financial entries rather than overwrite history.

Notification delivery should use an outbox pattern or equivalent durable retry design.

State transitions must be validated centrally and recorded in status history.

7. SMART BATCH PICKUP & RETURN — shared feature specification

Confirmed concept: allow customers in nearby areas to choose a collection time window. Clickit groups compatible individual orders into a route so a delivery partner can collect from multiple people in one trip. Example discussed: five customers with 5 kg, 3 kg, 4 kg, 2 kg and 5 kg of clothes; total 19 kg in the same 9:00–10:00 window.

7.1 Batch customer experience

Customer chooses an available pickup window.

App indicates whether the window is individual or shared-route eligible.

Customer sees the agreed price and any delivery fee before confirmation.

Backend may place the pickup request into a candidate group but does not promise a batch until operational rules are met.

Customer receives a notification when pickup is scheduled/partner assigned.

If the batch is split or delayed, customer receives an updated ETA and available alternatives.

7.2 Batch eligibility rules

Dimension

Rule to validate

Geography

Nearby H3 cells create candidate groups; road distance and route ETA determine actual compatibility.

Time window

Customer-approved windows must overlap or satisfy a published tolerance.

Capacity

Total parcel count, estimated weight and volume must fit partner/vehicle limits.

Destination

Worker/shop destinations and stop sequence must be compatible.

Handling

Keep each order labelled and physically segregated; apply category-specific handling.

Service promise

Do not silently move a customer outside the accepted time window.

Efficiency

Compare route cost and detour against separate trips; do not batch if it harms service.

Fallback

Permit individual route, smaller batch or manual dispatch when density is low.

7.3 Physical parcel integrity

Every booking has its own parcel or parcel set and unique tracking identifier.

Each bag/package is labelled with a non-guessable code; avoid exposing customer phone/address on external labels.

Record parcel count and seal/condition at each custody handoff.

Never combine separate customers' clothes or goods into an untraceable physical bundle.

Batch-level status never replaces stop-level and parcel-level status.

