8.1 ERD relationship rules

One user may have role-specific profiles; profile ownership must reference the same user identity.

Workers and services form a many-to-many relationship through worker_services.

One customer can create many bookings; each booking has its own customer, selected worker and address snapshot/reference.

One booking can have multiple items and quote versions.

One booking can create separate pickup and return delivery tasks.

One batch has many stops; one booking/task can have historical batch membership but only one active membership where policy requires.

One delivery task can have multiple assignment records over time; do not overwrite reassignment history.

One parcel has a unique tracking identity and multiple custody events.

One booking can have multiple payment attempts and provider events; successful financial capture must not duplicate.

Financial records should be append-only or reversal-based for auditability.

8.2 Constraints and indexes

Unique normalized phone number; unique provider event/transaction identifiers where applicable.

Check positive quantities and non-negative amounts; validate start/end timestamps.

Use foreign keys and deliberate deletion behaviour; retain business history through soft deactivation or archival.

Index booking customer/worker/status/date; task status/partner; batch time/status; service/category.

Use PostGIS GiST indexes for spatial queries and indexed H3 cell IDs for candidate grouping.

Use partial unique indexes for one active task assignment or one active batch membership where required.

Store money in integer minor units (paise) with currency code, or another explicitly consistent exact-decimal design.

Store booking-time snapshots of service name, unit, price and scope.

9. Security and privacy requirements

9.1 Authentication

Use phone OTP or another approved authentication method with rate limits, expiry, attempt caps and abuse monitoring.

Use short-lived access tokens and secure refresh/session rotation appropriate to the client platform.

Allow session revocation and logout from other devices.

Require MFA for privileged admin roles.

Never store passwords, OTPs, payment secrets or access tokens in plaintext.

9.2 Authorization

Apply role-based access control plus object-level checks on every record.

Customer can access only own bookings, addresses, payment records and support tickets.

Worker can access only own storefront and bookings assigned to that worker.

Partner can access only tasks offered/assigned to that partner and only necessary location/contact details.

Admin permissions must be separated by operational function and reviewed regularly.

Do not rely on hidden buttons or client-side checks; backend authorization is mandatory.

9.3 Data security

Use TLS for network traffic and managed encryption at rest for database, backups and object storage.

Store secrets in a server-side secret manager; never bundle private keys in mobile/web code.

Use private object storage and short-lived signed URLs for verification documents and proof images.

Mask phone numbers and exact addresses where full data is not needed.

Limit partner live location to active tasks/online operations with transparent notice and retention limits.

Set retention and deletion rules for location, OTP, identity evidence, support attachments and logs.

Scrub tokens, OTPs, addresses, payment details and identity data from application logs.

9.4 Application security

Validate inputs on the server and use parameterized database access.

Protect against SQL injection, XSS, CSRF where applicable, broken object-level authorization, replay and webhook spoofing.

Rate-limit OTP, authentication, search, booking, tracking and payment endpoints.

Use idempotency keys for retry-sensitive operations.

Run dependency scanning, secret scanning, code review and security testing before release.

Separate development, staging and production data, credentials and payment environments.

Back up the database and rehearse restoration.

9.5 Legal and compliance review

Before operating in India, obtain qualified legal, tax, privacy and payments advice for applicable data protection, consumer/e-commerce, GST/tax, worker/contractor, local operating and payment settlement requirements. This document is a product/technical specification, not legal advice or a compliance certification.

