11. Testing plan and acceptance criteria

11.1 Cross-application journey tests

Customer signs up, adds address, finds a worker and creates a booking.

Worker receives the request, accepts it and confirms a quote.

Customer approves the quote and completes payment.

Admin can locate the booking and see its full status timeline.

Delivery partner receives a task, accepts it, verifies pickup and records custody.

Worker confirms receipt, completes service and marks ready.

Partner collects return parcel and delivers it with proof.

Customer confirms receipt, sees receipt and submits a review.

Two or more customers join a compatible batch and each stop/parcel remains separately traceable.

One stop fails while the rest of the batch continues without losing successful events.

11.2 Security tests

Customer A cannot retrieve Customer B's booking by changing an ID.

Worker cannot view a different worker's jobs or private verification records.

Partner cannot see unassigned tasks' exact addresses or customer contact details.

Admin role cannot perform finance or super-admin actions without permission.

Expired/revoked token is denied.

Invalid or repeated payment webhook does not create duplicate capture/refund records.

Rate limits prevent OTP brute force and repeated booking/payment abuse.

Sensitive files cannot be accessed using expired or unauthorized URLs.

11.3 Operational tests

Worker rejects, customer cancels, partner rejects and customer is absent.

Parcel count mismatch and damaged item are recorded with evidence and escalation.

Batch exceeds vehicle capacity and is split safely.

Partner loses network during a stop and safely retries without duplicate custody events.

Payment provider is delayed or unavailable; booking state remains accurate.

Notification provider is unavailable; durable retry occurs without duplicate business actions.

